maya: Heads-up: intermittent JSON errors in node sample-app when reconnect happens mid-stream. Repro seems to show a keepalive/empty data event getting merged into the next data: payload.

raul: Which SDK + sample version?

maya: node-sdk v0.9.6, sample-app v1.4.0. Happen on macOS dev box and ubuntu CI runner. Browser SDK hasn't reproduced.

maya: Minimal repro: start a long streaming response, then simulate a network blip (toggle wifi or `tc qdisc`). After reconnect, client receives fragments where an empty `data:` event (heartbeat) ends up glued into the next JSON chunk as a literal `\n` and JSON.parse fails.

maya: Raw fragment capture (trimmed):

```
data: {"part":"one"}\n\ndata: {\n}\ndata: {"part":"two"}\n\n```

maya: Client-side concatenation sometimes produces `{\"part\":\"one\"}\n{\"part\":\"two\"}` which breaks.

cheng: Can you paste the sample-app parser?
\nmaya: Handler in sample-app (node):

```
let buf = '';
res.on('data', chunk => {
  buf += chunk.toString();
  const evs = buf.split('\n\n');
  buf = evs.pop() || '';
  evs.forEach(e => {
    const data = e.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:')).map(l => l.slice(5)).join('');
    try { handle(JSON.parse(data)); } catch (err) { console.error('parse failed', data); }
  });
});
```

cheng: Looks familiar. `.join('')` removes intended newlines between `data:` lines; when there is an explicit empty data event we should treat it as a heartbeat, not merge. Node's buffer boundaries can split anywhere, so we need a stateful assembler.

raul: Python SDK's parser keeps `data:` lines and joins them with `\n` preserving literal breaks; it also treats an all-whitespace data payload as heartbeat. Node should implement the same.

maya: Quick local patch that reduced failures:

```
const data = e.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:')).map(l => l.slice(5)).join('\n');
if (!data.trim()) { return; } // heartbeat
```

maya: Works better but I want canonical fix in the SDK.

